 "reviewHtml": "<div><p>We still have a month before spooky season arrives, but Chopova Lowena is, as always, one step ahead. The electric community spirit of the brand’s shows was as palpable as ever as guests bundled their way down narrow staircases to reach the cavernous bowling alleys of Bloomsbury Lanes on Saturday evening, winding their way past a witchy mise-en-scène of a rickety picket fence covered in cobwebs and into the underworld red glow of the indoor lighting. There were also buckets of fortune-cookie messages being handed out at the door—mine read: “I went to the Chopova Lowena show and all I got was HAUNTED!” <em>A-wooo!</em></p><p>This season, Emma Chopova and Laura Lowena fell down a rabbit hole of Slavic folklore: the woodland witch Baba Yaga, the house fairy Kikimora, the winter goddess Marzanna, and some monsters and mermaids thrown in for good measure. Each figure had its own section of the show—modular aprons and dresses inspired by Polish folk costumes and laser-cut chiffon flower sleeves for the witches, shaggy patchworked faux fur and deadstock toweling for the beasts, and a final series of very pretty bias-cut skirts and dresses for the elven fairies. (All of this was brought to life through some brilliantly inventive hair and makeup—gel tears running down the face, metal teeth inspired by Baba Yaga, pearlescent skin—by Kiyoko Odo and Lauren Reynolds, respectively.) For Chopova, who was raised by Bulgarian parents in the US, these tall tales have always felt very real. “The superstitions are so deeply ingrained,” she said before the show. “When I was growing up, my mom would sort of scare me with them. They’re very much alive, and there are still festivals held around these characters.”</p><p>Of course, there was also a sporty element factored in: Here, bowling. (Hence the fact that models were stomping their way down the lanes, gingerly stepping across the barrier to avoid becoming runway gutter balls.) After discovering the story of Marion Ladewig, a 1950s housewife turned pro bowler, they had their second ingredient for the collection, which manifested in the form of silhouettes from that decade—big circle skirts, drop waists, lopsided takes on bouffant hairstyles—as well as bomber jackets that allowed plenty of arm rotation for a wearer to hit a powerful strike. As ever, there was some ingenious casting here too: The crowd was particularly enamored with a pair of 60-something twins, Joanna and Julia, who came out one after the other. Well, until the real stars of the show arrived: a tiny Chihuahua wrapped up in a neon green puffer jacket to be carried down the runway, followed by a schnauzer trotting along on a vibrantly braided leash in a pair of black goggles (doggles?). The model had to offer him a treat to get him to leap across the bowling lane gutters—when he did, the crowd went wild.</p><p>And the crowd went wild at the end of the show too, with many attendees choosing to hang out in the space afterward and admire each other’s vintage-inspired knits and punky carabiner skirts. The word <em>community</em> gets overused in reference to fashion brands, but in Chopova Lowena’s case, it rings entirely true—every show feels like a kind of reunion of their obsessive acolytes. Except now, they don’t have to wait six months to see one another again: In August, the pair opened their first brick-and-mortar store, in Bethnal Green, named the Chopova Lowena Clubhouse, which arrived with riotous interiors designed by the duo and Chopova’s artist and designer boyfriend, David Pringle. There are Polish pine cupboards fitted with peephole video screens, Bulgarian tapestries in the fitting rooms, and even a “skirt bar” slathered in vintage stickers, where you can commission your choice of fabrics and hardware to get a custom carabiner skirt of your own. It’s been a roaring success: “It’s been incredible—I think it’s going to be a huge supporting factor of the business,” said Chopova. “It’s really changing the game already.” Added Lowena, “It’s giving us more flexibility to be creative in different ways and to sell less seasonally, which we really like.”</p><p>Part of the store’s philosophy is to offer more accessibly priced items too—Chopova says she was inspired by teenage visits to the Marc Jacobs stores in New York, where you could pick up a $50 T-shirt or a pocket mirror even if you couldn’t afford the main-line pieces—and so they decided to throw a few of those items into the collection as well. (One model stepped out with Chopova Lowena Clubhouse stickers on her face and toting shopping bags as if she’d just raided the store.) Chopova and Lowena’s maverick spirit always ensures that, within their collections, there really is something for everyone.</p></div>",
